  <page>

     <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Calculate the following limits.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}.</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that <m>\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}</m> is not defined at <m>x=1</m>, so we cannot simply plug in <m>x=1</m>.
                    Note that <m>x^2-1= (x-1)(x+1)</m>.
                    Thus, if <m>x\neq 1</m>,
                    <md>
                        \frac{x^2-1}{x-1}= \frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}=x+1.
                    </md>
                    Hence,
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{x^2-1}{x-1} = \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}(x+1)=1+1 =2.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Since <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 1}x^2-1 = 0</m> and <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 1}x-1=0</m>, we can also answer this question using L'Hospital's rule.
                    We had not covered L'Hosptal's rule when we took this exam, so this was not the approach that was expected of you.
                    Proceeding, we have
                    <md>
                    <mrow>  \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{x^2-1}{x-1} \amp \overset{\text{L'Hospital}}{=} \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-1)}{\frac{d}{dx}(x-1)}  </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{2x}{1} = 2. </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}x^2 \sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right).</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note <m>-1\leq \sin(1/x)\leq 1</m> for all <m>x \neq 0</m>.
                    Multiplying across by <m>x^2</m> (which is never negative), we have
                    <md>
                        -x^2 \leq x^2 \sin(1/x) \leq x^2.
                    </md>
                    Since <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} x^2 = 0 = \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} -x^2</m>, it follows by the <em>Squeeze theorem</em> that
                    <md>
                        \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}x^2 \sin(1/x) = 0.
                    </md>

                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow \infty}\frac{2x^3 - 7x +5}{2x^3 +3x^2 + 1}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We will multiply the top and bottom of the quotient by <m>1/x^3</m>, since <m>x^3</m> is the highest power of <m>x</m> in the denominator.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\lim_{x \rightarrow \infty}\frac{2x^3 - 7x +5}{2x^3 +3x^2 + 1} \amp= \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty}\frac{(2x^3 - 7x +5)\frac{1}{x^3}}{(2x^3 +3x^2 + 1)\frac{1}{x^3}} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{2 - \frac{7}{x^2} + \frac{5}{x^3}}{2 + \frac{3}{x}+ \frac{1}{x^3}} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{2 - 0 + 0}{2 + 0 + 0}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{2}{2}=1. </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Give the derivative of the each of the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.75">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>y = \frac{x^3}{2-x^2}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the derivative we will use the quotient rule.
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{(2-x^2)\frac{d}{dx}(x^3) - x^3 \frac{d}{dx}(2-x^2)}{(2-x^2)^2} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{3x^2(2-x^2)-x^3(-2x)}{(2-x^2)^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{6x^2-x^4}{(2-x^2)^2}.</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task  workspace="0.75">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>g(x) = 5\sqrt{x}(x^2 + x)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We can rewrite <m>g(x)</m> as
                    <md>
                        g(x) = 5 (x^{5/2} + x^{3/2}).
                    </md>
                    Thus
                    <md>
                        g'(x) = 5 \left(\frac{5}{2}x^{3/2} + \frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\right).
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We can also answer this question using the product rule.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>g'(x) \amp =5\left(\sqrt{x}(2x+1) + \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}(x^2 +x)\right) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 5 \left(\frac{5}{2}x^{3/2} + \frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\right). </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>



        <task  workspace="0.75">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x) = \sin(x^2)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the derivative, we will use the chain rule.
                    <md>
                        f'(x)=\cos(x^2) (2x) = 2x\cos(x^2).
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Implicitly differentiate the following equation to find <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>.
                <md>
                    y\cos(x) = 2x^2 + 4y^2.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We have that
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(y\cos(x)) = \frac{d}{dx}(2x^2 + 4y^2).
                </md>
                Calculating the left-hand size we get
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(y\cos(x)) = \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) + y(-\sin(x))= \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) -y \sin(x).
                </md>
                And calculating the right-hand size we get
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(2x^2 + 4y^2) = 4x + 8y \frac{dy}{dx}.
                </md>
                Setting the left-hand side equal to the right-hand side, we get
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) -y \sin(x) = 4x + 8y \frac{dy}{dx}.
                </md>
                Solving for <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>, we get
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{4x + y\sin(x)}{\cos(x) - 8y}.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The radius of a sphere is increasing at a rate of 4 mm/s. How fast is the volume increasing when the diameter is 10 mm?
            </p>
            <p>
                Recall, the volume <m>V</m> of a sphere of radius <m>r</m> is given by <m>V=\frac{4}{3}\pi r^3</m> mm<m>^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The rate of change of the volume with respect to time <m>t</m> is
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt} = \frac{4}{3}\pi 3 r^2 \frac{dr}{dt} = 4\pi r^2 \frac{dr}{dt}.
                </md>
                We are told that <m>\frac{dr}{dt} = 4</m>.
                Thus
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt} = 16 \pi r^2.
                </md>
                When the diameter is <m>10</m>mm, the radius <m>r = 5</m>. 
                Hence, when the diameter is <m>10</m>, the rate of change of the volume is 
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dt} = 16 \pi 5^2 = 400\pi \text{mm}^3\text{/sec}.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt{1+3x}</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the linearization <m>L(x)</m> of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x = 5.</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The linearization <m>L(x)</m> is given by the formula
                    <md>
                        L(x) = f'(5)(x-5) + f(5).
                    </md>
                    Calculating the derivative, 
                    <md>
                        f'(x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{1+3x}}(3) = \frac{3}{2\sqrt{1+3x}}.
                    </md>
                    We have that
                    <md>
                        f(5) = \sqrt{1+15}=4 \text{ and } f'(5)=\frac{3}{2\sqrt{1+15}}=\frac{3}{8},
                    </md>
                    and so
                    <md>
                        L(x) = \frac{3}{8}(x-5) + 4.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Approximate <m>f(5.1)</m> (using the linearization found in part (a)).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        f(5.1) \approx L(5.1),
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        L(5.1) = \frac{3}{8}(5.1-5)+ 4 = \frac{3}{80}+4 = \frac{323}{80}.
                    </md>
                    Thus
                    <md>
                        f(5.1) \approx \frac{323}{80} = 4.0375.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Differentiate the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.75">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>y = \ln(x^2 - 6x + 1)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that, <m>\frac{d}{dx}(\ln(x))=\frac{1}{x}</m>, and <m>\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-6x+1) = 2x -6</m>.
                    Thus, by the chain rule,
                    <md>
                        \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{x^2 - 6x +1}(2x - 6) = \frac{2x-6}{x^2 - 6x +1}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x) = \sin^{-1}(x^2)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that <m>\frac{d}{dx}(\sin^{-1}(x)) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</m>, and <m>\frac{d}{dx}(x^2)=2x</m>.
                    Thus, by the chain rule,
                    <md>
                        f'(x) = \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-(x^2)^2}}\right)(2x) = \frac{2x}{\sqrt{1-x^4}}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>h(x) = e^{x^2-x}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    By the chain rule,
                    <md>
                        h'(x) = (2x-1)e^{x^2-x}.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>


    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Below is the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> for <m>x</m> between <m>-2</m> and <m>4</m>.
                That is, <m>x</m> is in the interval <m>[-2,4].</m>
                Answer the following questions based on the graph.
            </p>

            <p>
                <image width="30%">
                <sageplot label="midterm2_2">
                    rad = 0.05
                    disp= 0.02
                    f(x) = (abs(x-2))^(2/3) + 2
                    h(x) = 2*x+2

                    plt1 = plot(f,(0+rad,3-rad), gridlines='major')
                    plt3 = plot(f,(3+rad,4))
                    plt2 = plot(h,(-2,0))
                    
                    pt1 = circle((0,f(0)),rad, fill=False)
                    pt2 = circle((0,2),rad, fill=True)
                    pt3 = circle((3,3),rad, fill=False)

                    plt = plt1 + plt2 + plt3 +pt1 + pt2 + pt3
                    plt.set_axes_range(-2,4,-2,4)
                    plt
                </sageplot>

                </image>    
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    At which <m>x</m>-values is <m>f</m> <em>not</em> continuous?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The function <m>f(x)</m> is not continuous at
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <m>x = 0</m> (a <em>jump</em> discontinuity); and
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <m>x = 3</m> (a <em>removable</em> discontinuity).
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    At which <m>x</m>-values is <m>f</m> <em>not</em> differentiable?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The function <m>f</m> is not differentiable at
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <m>x = 0</m> and <m>x = 3</m> (since <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=0</m> or <m>x = 3</m>); and
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <m>x = 2</m> (since <m>f(x)</m> has a cusp at <m>x=2</m>.)
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On which intervals is the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> positive?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f'(x)</m> is positive where <m>f(x)</m> is increasing. Thus, <m>f'(x)</m> is positive on the intervals
                    <md>
                        (-2,0) \cup (2,3) \cup (3,4).
                    </md>                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On which intervals is the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> negative?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f'(x)</m> is negative where <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing. Thus, <m>f'(x)</m> is negative on the interval
                    <md>
                        (0,2).
                    </md>                    
                </p>

                <figure>
                    <caption>Graph of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f'(x).</m></caption>
                    <image width="75%">
                        <shortdescription>Graph of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f'(x)</m></shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Graph of <m>f(x)</m> in blue and <m>f'(x)</m> in blue. The red curve is positive where the blue curve is increasing; the red curve is negative where the blue curve is decreasing. The red curve has a vertical asymptote at <m>x=2</m> since the blue curve has a cusp at <m>x=2</m>.</p></description>
                        <sageplot label="midterm2_sol2">
                            rad = 0.05
                            disp= 0.02
                            f(x) = (abs(x-2))^(2/3) + 2
                            h(x) = 2*x+2
                            g(x) = 2
                            k(x) = (2/3)*(abs(x-2))^(-1/3)

                            plt1 = plot(f,(0+rad,3-rad), gridlines='major', legend_label="$f(x)$")
                            plt3 = plot(f,(3+rad,4))
                            plt2 = plot(h,(-2,0))

                            plt4 = plot(g,(-2,0), color="red", legend_label="$f'(x)$")
                            plt5 = plot(-k,(0+rad,2), color="red")
                            plt6 = plot(k,(3+rad,4), color="red")
                            plt7 = plot(k,(2,3-rad), color="red")
                            
                            pt1 = circle((0,f(0)),rad, fill=False)
                            pt2 = circle((0,2),rad, fill=True)
                            pt3 = circle((3,3),rad, fill=False)

                            pt4 = circle((0,2),rad, fill=False, color="red")
                            pt5 = circle((3,k(3)),rad, fill=False, color="red")
                            pt6 = circle((0,-k(0)),rad, fill=False, color="red")

                            plt = plt1 + plt2 + plt3 +pt1 + pt2 + pt3 + plt4 + plt5 +plt6 +plt7 + pt4 + pt5 + pt6
                            plt.set_axes_range(-2,4,-2,4)
                            plt
                        </sageplot>
                    </image>
                </figure>

            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

    <page>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                On the axes below, sketch a function <m>f(x)</m> which satisfies the following conditions.
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="50% 50%" margins="auto" valign="top">
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        The domain of <m>f</m> is <m>(-\infty,3) \cup (3,\infty)</m>;
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=-1</m> or <m>x=3</m>, <m>f</m> is continuous at all other values of <m>x</m>;
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow 3^{-}}f(x)=-\infty</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow 3^{+}}f(x)=-\infty</m>
                    </li>

                </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <m>f(-1)=1</m>
                    </li>
                    
                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^{+}}f(x)=2</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -1^{-}}f(x)=3</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow \infty}f(x)=2</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>\lim_{x \rightarrow -\infty}f(x)=-\infty</m>
                    </li>
                    
                </ul>
                </p>
                
            </sidebyside>
                        <p>
                <image>
                    <sageplot label="midterm2_3">
                        a = plot([])
                        a.set_axes_range(-7,7,-7,7)
                        a
                    </sageplot>
                </image>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                There is no one right answer to this question.
                The following is an example of a correct answer.
                The vertical and horizontal asymptotes are illustrated with dotted lines; the function is <m>f</m> is graphed in blue.
            </p>
            <p>
                <image>
                    <sageplot label="midterm2_sol3">
                        rad = 0.05
                        disp= 0.02
                        p=1/5
                        f(x) = -1/(x-3)^2
                        h(x) = -(x+1)^2+3
                        a = plot(f + 2.0625, (-1+rad,3)) + plot(h,(-7,-1-rad)) + plot(f+2,(3,7))

                        pt1 = circle((-1,3), rad, fill=False)
                        pt2 = circle((-1,2), rad, fill=False)
                        pt3 = circle((-1,1), rad, fill=True)

                        L = line([(3,-8),(3,8)], linestyle="--", color="green", thickness="0.75")
                        M = line([(3.5,2),(8,2)], linestyle="--", color="green", thickness="0.75")

                        a = a + pt1 + pt2 + pt3 + L +M
                        a.set_axes_range(-7,7,-7,7)
                        a
                    </sageplot>
                </image>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>